capture/14069463/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "eb005120cea2be0499d5eaa05b3b89efd56836d2e3b8ccaa7a5b9679bac0fd21"} {"question" "q-97516" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/state.json" "file_sha256" "be737d4a4ca1e1de45387dc36174cf8d0bfe6795fc7f8a2de868752a93d7cbfd"} {"question" "q-97516" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.0784"] ["math" "0.0988"] ["math" "0.1015"] ["math" "0.1319"] ["math" "0.1523"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/state.json" "file_sha256" "be737d4a4ca1e1de45387dc36174cf8d0bfe6795fc7f8a2de868752a93d7cbfd"} {"question" "q-97516" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.0988"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/state.json" "file_sha256" "be737d4a4ca1e1de45387dc36174cf8d0bfe6795fc7f8a2de868752a93d7cbfd"} {"question" "q-279767" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $X∼B(5,0.1),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places** $P(0<X\\le 2)≈${{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/state.json" "file_sha256" "be737d4a4ca1e1de45387dc36174cf8d0bfe6795fc7f8a2de868752a93d7cbfd"} {"question" "q-279767" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(5,0.1),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.1)^{x}(0.9)^{5-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0<X\\le 2) & =P(X\\in {1,2}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1})(0.1)^{1}(0.9)^{5-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2})(0.1)^{2}(0.9)^{5-2} \\\\  & =5(0.1)^{1}(0.9)^{4}+10(0.1)^{2}(0.9)^{3} \\\\  & ≈0.401\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/state.json" "file_sha256" "be737d4a4ca1e1de45387dc36174cf8d0bfe6795fc7f8a2de868752a93d7cbfd"} {"question" "q-279767" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "eb005120cea2be0499d5eaa05b3b89efd56836d2e3b8ccaa7a5b9679bac0fd21"} {"question" "q-279767" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/state.json" "file_sha256" "be737d4a4ca1e1de45387dc36174cf8d0bfe6795fc7f8a2de868752a93d7cbfd"} {"question" "q-279767" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.401"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/state.json" "file_sha256" "be737d4a4ca1e1de45387dc36174cf8d0bfe6795fc7f8a2de868752a93d7cbfd"} {"question" "q-279984" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $X∼B(7,0.8),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X<6)≈${{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/state.json" "file_sha256" "be737d4a4ca1e1de45387dc36174cf8d0bfe6795fc7f8a2de868752a93d7cbfd"} {"question" "q-279984" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(7,0.8),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.8)^{x}(0.2)^{7-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X<6)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0,1,2,…,5.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X\\ge 6)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=6,7,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X\\ge 6),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 6) & =P(X\\in {6,7}) \\\\  & =f(6)+f(7) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{6})(0.8)^{6}(0.2)^{7-6}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{7})(0.8)^{7}(0.2)^{7-7} \\\\  & =7(0.8)^{6}(0.2)+(0.8)^{7} \\\\  & ≈0.577\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<6) & =1-P(X\\ge 6) \\\\  & ≈1-0.577 \\\\  & =0.423\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/state.json" "file_sha256" "be737d4a4ca1e1de45387dc36174cf8d0bfe6795fc7f8a2de868752a93d7cbfd"} {"question" "q-279984" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "eb005120cea2be0499d5eaa05b3b89efd56836d2e3b8ccaa7a5b9679bac0fd21"} {"question" "q-279984" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/state.json" "file_sha256" "be737d4a4ca1e1de45387dc36174cf8d0bfe6795fc7f8a2de868752a93d7cbfd"} {"question" "q-279984" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.423"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/state.json" "file_sha256" "be737d4a4ca1e1de45387dc36174cf8d0bfe6795fc7f8a2de868752a93d7cbfd"} {"question" "q-279981" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $X∼B(6,0.9),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X<5)≈${{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/state.json" "file_sha256" "be737d4a4ca1e1de45387dc36174cf8d0bfe6795fc7f8a2de868752a93d7cbfd"} {"question" "q-279981" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(6,0.9),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.9)^{x}(0.1)^{6-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X<5)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0,1,2,3,4.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X\\ge 5)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=5,6,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X\\ge 5),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 5) & =P(X\\in {5,6}) \\\\  & =f(5)+f(6) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{5})(0.9)^{5}(0.1)^{6-5}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{6})(0.9)^{6}(0.1)^{6-6} \\\\  & =6(0.9)^{5}(0.1)+(0.9)^{6} \\\\  & ≈0.886\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =1-P(X\\ge 5) \\\\  & ≈1-0.886 \\\\  & ≈0.114\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/state.json" "file_sha256" "be737d4a4ca1e1de45387dc36174cf8d0bfe6795fc7f8a2de868752a93d7cbfd"} {"question" "q-279981" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "eb005120cea2be0499d5eaa05b3b89efd56836d2e3b8ccaa7a5b9679bac0fd21"} {"question" "q-279981" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/state.json" "file_sha256" "be737d4a4ca1e1de45387dc36174cf8d0bfe6795fc7f8a2de868752a93d7cbfd"} {"question" "q-279981" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.114"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/state.json" "file_sha256" "be737d4a4ca1e1de45387dc36174cf8d0bfe6795fc7f8a2de868752a93d7cbfd"} {"question" "q-281208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $X∼B(12,\\frac{2}{3}),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X\\le 11)≈${{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/state.json" "file_sha256" "be737d4a4ca1e1de45387dc36174cf8d0bfe6795fc7f8a2de868752a93d7cbfd"} {"question" "q-281208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(12,\\frac{2}{3}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{x}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{12-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X\\le 11)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0,1,2,…,11.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X>11)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=12,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X>11),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>11) & =P(X\\in {12}) \\\\  & =f(12) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{12}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{12}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{12-12} \\\\  & =1{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{12}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.008\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 11) & =1-P(X>11) \\\\  & ≈1-0.008 \\\\  & =0.992\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/state.json" "file_sha256" "be737d4a4ca1e1de45387dc36174cf8d0bfe6795fc7f8a2de868752a93d7cbfd"} {"question" "q-281208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "eb005120cea2be0499d5eaa05b3b89efd56836d2e3b8ccaa7a5b9679bac0fd21"} {"question" "q-281208" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/state.json" "file_sha256" "be737d4a4ca1e1de45387dc36174cf8d0bfe6795fc7f8a2de868752a93d7cbfd"} {"question" "q-281208" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.992"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/state.json" "file_sha256" "be737d4a4ca1e1de45387dc36174cf8d0bfe6795fc7f8a2de868752a93d7cbfd"} {"question" "q-97521" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $X∼B(7,0.5),$ compute $P(X\\le 6).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/state.json" "file_sha256" "be737d4a4ca1e1de45387dc36174cf8d0bfe6795fc7f8a2de868752a93d7cbfd"} {"question" "q-97521" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(7,0.5),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.5)^{x}(0.5)^{7-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X\\le 6)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0,1,2,…,6.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X>6)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=7,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X>6),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>6) & =P(X\\in {7}) \\\\  & =f(7) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{7})(0.5)^{7}(0.5)^{7-7} \\\\  & =(0.5)^{7} \\\\  & ≈0.0078\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 6) & =1-P(X>6) \\\\  & ≈1-0.0078 \\\\  & =0.9922\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/state.json" "file_sha256" "be737d4a4ca1e1de45387dc36174cf8d0bfe6795fc7f8a2de868752a93d7cbfd"} {"question" "q-97521" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "eb005120cea2be0499d5eaa05b3b89efd56836d2e3b8ccaa7a5b9679bac0fd21"} {"question" "q-97521" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/state.json" "file_sha256" "be737d4a4ca1e1de45387dc36174cf8d0bfe6795fc7f8a2de868752a93d7cbfd"} {"question" "q-97521" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.9078"] ["math" "0.9375"] ["math" "0.9547"] ["math" "0.9625"] ["math" "0.99