\  & =1{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{7}+7{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{6}+21{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5} \\\\  & ≈0.227\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & =1-P(X<3) \\\\  & ≈1-0.227 \\\\  & =0.773\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/state.json" "file_sha256" "be737d4a4ca1e1de45387dc36174cf8d0bfe6795fc7f8a2de868752a93d7cbfd"} {"question" "q-279858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "eb005120cea2be0499d5eaa05b3b89efd56836d2e3b8ccaa7a5b9679bac0fd21"} {"question" "q-279858" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/state.json" "file_sha256" "be737d4a4ca1e1de45387dc36174cf8d0bfe6795fc7f8a2de868752a93d7cbfd"} {"question" "q-279858" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.773"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/state.json" "file_sha256" "be737d4a4ca1e1de45387dc36174cf8d0bfe6795fc7f8a2de868752a93d7cbfd"} {"question" "e-8557" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $X \\sim B(6, 0.7),$ compute $P(X = 2).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/example-8557.json" "file_sha256" "e2b3df66469bda9aebc0d2f8eabeca47690143aea69326f8e6f5c9b58cfdfe19"} {"question" "e-8557" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X \\sim B(6, 0.7),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{6}{x} (0.7)^x (0.3)^{6-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2) & =f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{2})(0.7)^{2}(0.3)^{6-2} \\\\  & =15(0.7)^{2}(0.3)^{4} \\\\  & ≈0.0595\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/example-8557.json" "file_sha256" "e2b3df66469bda9aebc0d2f8eabeca47690143aea69326f8e6f5c9b58cfdfe19"} {"question" "e-8564" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $X \\sim B(10, 0.7),$ compute $P(X > 8).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/example-8564.json" "file_sha256" "13f05d7c8e9c6cfda12eea07603d86d4ccfa7489d1455699cb633f6dea2d51d6"} {"question" "e-8564" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X \\sim B(10, 0.7),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{10}{x} (0.7)^x (0.3)^{10-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>8) & =P(X\\in {9,10}) \\\\  & =f(9)+f(10) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{9})(0.7)^{9}(0.3)^{10-9}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{10})(0.7)^{10}(0.3)^{10-10} \\\\  & =10(0.7)^{9}(0.3)+1(0.7)^{10}(0.3)^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.1493\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/example-8564.json" "file_sha256" "13f05d7c8e9c6cfda12eea07603d86d4ccfa7489d1455699cb633f6dea2d51d6"} {"question" "e-8558" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given $X \\sim B(5, 0.3),$ compute $P(0 <     X < 3).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/example-8558.json" "file_sha256" "3e6a001c730b7ef8ba09ff3a5e6d2691d4cdd25ed6d73d8e2c5247a8b2d84e16"} {"question" "e-8558" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X \\sim B(5, 0.3),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{5}{x} (0.3)^x (0.7)^{5-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0<X<3) & =P(X\\in {1,2}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1})(0.3)^{1}(0.7)^{5-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2})(0.3)^{2}(0.7)^{5-2} \\\\  & =5(0.3)(0.7)^{4}+10(0.3)^{2}(0.7)^{3} \\\\  & ≈0.6689\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nto $4$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/example-8558.json" "file_sha256" "3e6a001c730b7ef8ba09ff3a5e6d2691d4cdd25ed6d73d8e2c5247a8b2d84e16"} {"question" "e-8559" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $X \\sim B(7, 0.2),$ compute $P(X \\geq 2).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/example-8559.json" "file_sha256" "375d6e286d1401c0de23521f478d11ae260f1c8ea9f3816c071dcdb9e8a0dcf0"} {"question" "e-8559" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X \\sim B(7, 0.2),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{7}{x} (0.2)^x (0.8)^{7-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X \\geq 2)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=2, 3, 4, \\ldots, 7.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X < 2)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0, 1,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X < 2),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<2) & =P(X\\in {0,1}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{0})(0.2)^{0}(0.8)^{7-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{1})(0.2)^{1}(0.8)^{7-1} \\\\  & =(0.8)^{7}+7(0.2)(0.8)^{6} \\\\  & ≈0.5767\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & ≈1-0.5767 \\\\  & =0.4233\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069463/example-8559.json" "file_sha256" "375d6e286d1401c0de23521f478d11ae260f1c8ea9f3816c071dcdb9e8a0dcf0"}] "usage" {"e-8559" [] "q-96728" [] "q-97516" [] "e-8564" [] "e-8558" [] "e-8557" []} "basis" 2374 "retractions" [[17592186167417 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nGiven that $X∼B(4, 0.2)$, compute $P(X = 3)$. Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places."] [17592186167448 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nGiven $X∼B(6, 0.2)$, compute $P(3 \\le X \\le 5)$. Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places."] [17592186166601 :question/problem "Given that $X \\sim B(6, 0.7)$, compute $P(X = 2)$. Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places."] [17592186166601 :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p)$, where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X \\sim B(6, 0.7)$, so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\displaystyle \\binom{6}{x} (0.7)^x (0.3)^{6-x}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = 2) &= f(2) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{6}{2})(0.7)^{2}(0.3)^{6 - 2} \\\\\n&= 15(0.7)^{2}(0.3)^{4} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.0595\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places."] [17592186166604 :question/problem "Given that $X \\sim B(10, 0.7)$, compute $P(X > 8)$. Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places."] [17592186166604 :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p)$, where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X \\sim B(10, 0.7)$, so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\displaystyle \\binom{10}{x} (0.7)^x (0.3)^{10-x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X > 8) &= P(X ∈ {9, 10}) \\\\\n&= f(9) + f(10) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{10}{9})(0.7)^{9}(0.3)^{10 - 9} + (\\frac{10}{10})(0.7)^{10}(0.3)^{10 - 10} \\\\\n&= 10(0.7)^{9}(0.3) + 1(0.7)^{10}(0.3)^{0} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.1493\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places."] [17592186166602 :question/problem "Given $X \\sim B(5, 0.3)$, compute $P(0 < X < 3)$. Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places."] [17592186166602 :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p)$, where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X \\sim B(5, 0.3)$, so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\displaystyle \\binom{5}{x} (0.3)^x (0.7)^{5-x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(0 < X < 3) &= P(X ∈ {1, 2}) \\\\\n&= f(1) + f(2) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{5}{1})(0.3)^{1}(0.7)^{5 - 1} + (\\frac{5}{2})(0.3)^{2}(0.7)^{5 - 2} \\\\\n&= 5(0.3)(0.7)^{4} + 10(0.3)^{2}(0.7)^{3} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.6689\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places."] [17592186166603 :question/problem "Given that $X \\sim B(7, 0.2)$, compute $P(X \\geq 2)$. Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places."] [17592186166603 :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p)$, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X \\sim B(7, 0.2)$, so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\displaystyle \\binom{7}{x} (0.2)^x (0.8)^{7-x}\n$$\n\nComputing\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq 2)\n$$\n\nwill require us to compute $f(x)$ at\n\n$$\nx=2, 3, 4, \\ldots, 7\n$$\n\nHowever, if we compute the complement $P(X < 2)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0, 1$, which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X < 2)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X < 2) &= P(X ∈ {0, 1}) \\\\\n&= f(0) + f(1) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{7}{0})(0.2)^{0}(0.8)^{7 - 0} + (\\frac{7}{1})(0.2)^{1}(0.8)^{7 - 1} \\\\\n&= (0.8)^{7} + 7(0.2)(0.8)^{6} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.5767\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\ge 2) &= 1 - P(X < 2) \\\\\n&\\approx 1 - 0.5767 \\\\\n&= 0.4233\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186166051 17592186166052 17592186166053 17592186166054 17592186166055 17592186166237 17592186166238 17592186166239 17592186166240 17592186166241] "immutable_fields" [[17592186166050 :answer-field/correct] [17592186166050 :answer-field/id] [17592186166050 :answer-field/key] [17592186166050 :answer-field/type] [17592186166236 :answer-f